1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
A rocket can climb toward space.

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.240
But the engine for its final push is beyond your reach.

3
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.360
Not because physics forbids it.

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.480
Because the technology is not being transferred.

5
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.600
So what do you do when the one engine you need is the one you cannot get?

6
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:33.820
In the early nineteen nineties, India faced restrictions around acquiring cryogenic rocket technology.

7
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:42.040
It was an uncomfortable message.

8
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:50.260
The machine existed.

9
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:58.480
Other nations had mastered it.

10
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:06.700
But access was another matter.

11
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.983
Why is a cryogenic engine so difficult?

12
00:01:11.983 --> 00:01:17.267
Start with a kitchen gas stove.

13
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:22.550
Fuel meets oxygen, burns, and releases energy.

14
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:27.833
A rocket needs that reaction to happen far more intensely, while carrying its own oxygen.

15
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:33.117
A cryogenic stage uses propellants kept at extraordinarily low temperatures.

16
00:01:33.117 --> 00:01:38.400
The cold makes them useful, but it also makes the plumbing unforgiving.

17
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.000
Now picture a bicycle pump.

18
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.600
Each push forces air through a narrow passage.

19
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.200
A rocket engine needs a continuous, enormous flow of propellants.

20
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.800
Tiny hand pumps cannot do it.

21
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.400
Powerful turbopumps spin rapidly to push fuel and oxidiser into a combustion chamber.

22
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.000
The pump is not an accessory.

23
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.600
Without the right pressure and flow, the whole engine fails.

24
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:16.333
Then comes the chamber.

25
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:22.067
Imagine a pressure cooker, except the goal is not to trap hot gas.

26
00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:27.800
The goal is to send it racing through a shaped nozzle.

27
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:33.533
The exhaust accelerates backward, and the rocket gains forward momentum.

28
00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:39.267
But the chamber must survive intense heat while the propellants arriving nearby are extremely cold.

29
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:45.000
Materials, seals, cooling passages, valves: everything has to cooperate.

30
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:51.667
That is why copying the outline of an engine is not the same as building one.

31
00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:58.333
You must understand the invisible behaviour inside it.

32
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:05.000
You must test, discover what failed, change the design, and test again.

33
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:11.950
India's space programme took that long route.

34
00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:18.900
Early flights of the GSLV rocket used cryogenic stages supplied from abroad.

35
00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:25.850
In January twenty fourteen, a GSLV flew with an indigenous cryogenic stage for the first time.

36
00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:32.800
Later, a different indigenous engine, the CE twenty, took over the upper stage of the heavier LVM three rocket.

37
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:39.750
According to ISRO, it has performed in eight successive LVM three missions, Chandrayaan three among them.

38
00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:46.700
In July twenty twenty-six, its flight acceptance test reached twenty-two tonnes of thrust.

39
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:53.650
The point is not a single triumphant test.

40
00:03:53.650 --> 00:04:00.600
It is an industrial capability that can be repeated.

41
00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.317
Here is a fictional scenario.

42
00:04:05.317 --> 00:04:10.033
A rocket is ready.

43
00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:14.750
Its upper-stage pump delivers slightly too little pressure.

44
00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:19.467
The combustion chamber cannot receive the flow it needs.

45
00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:24.183
The vehicle may be beautifully assembled, but its mission is now in danger.

46
00:04:24.183 --> 00:04:28.900
That is what engineering independence really demands: reliability in every hidden part.

47
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:37.800
The story is not finished.

48
00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:46.700
Higher performance, more capable stages, manufacturing consistency and future propulsion systems still require work.

49
00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:55.600
And international cooperation remains valuable.

50
00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:02.067
That difference matters.

51
00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:08.533
A nation does not become capable because it declares itself capable.

52
00:05:08.533 --> 00:05:15.000
It becomes capable when an engine works under conditions that cannot be negotiated.
